A||||16||fiero56||jerky hesitation||||5||
Z||000000||fiero56||06-19-2000||01:11 PM||||The car: '88 Formula(v-6) 5-speed<P>The problem: under lightly moderate load, <BR>the car "hesitates". Hesitates really isn't the right word for it though. It j-e-r-k-s really quickly, several times a second. No weird noises are made, except it makes the low pitched "thwup" sound and engine makes when it misses. The engine makes this sound every time it j-e-r-k-s(several times a second). The engine does this anytime a slight moderate load is placed on it, and does it all the up through WOT. While this "missing" is ocurring, there is very little power, making it very difficult to accelerate.<P>The clutch slipping has pretty much been eliminated. It has around 15,000 miles on it, and works great. I drove the car up to 15mph, and put it in 3rd gear(900rpm)and hit the gas. It still hesitated like crazy. I don't think the clutch could slip at such a low rpm. So I think drivetrain is eliminated.<P>Any ideas or solutions are greatly appreciated.<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 06-19-2000).]||||reg||5
Z||000001||Gold-86SE||06-19-2000||02:11 PM||||<BR>Is your SES light on while driving? If yes, get the codes from the computer.<P>Check and/or replace the following:<P>fuel filter<BR>plugs and plug wires<BR>distributor cap and rotor<BR>timing<P>Keep us up-to-date.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by Gold-86SE (edited 06-19-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||dman||06-19-2000||02:48 PM||||Hmmm...just a guess but check your MAP sensor. That is what its for, it checks when you're under load. Check the vacuum lines that run to it. It is on the right side of your upper intake plenum. Disconnect it and see if it runs differently. If there is no change than its probably bad. Replacement cost should be around $25 to $40 US. On a properly functioning car, disconnecting the map sensor would make it run like crap.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000003||fiero56||06-19-2000||03:25 PM||||Figure I will add this too.<P>First, no the SES light hasn't come on. Wish it had, I love it when the computer tells me whats wrong. No codes stored. I checked just in case.<P>I did a visual run-over of all vac lines, nothing looked bad, but I will grab a can of something flamable(wd-40 or similar)and spray them down. The car still idles beautifully, but if I smack the gas real fast at idle out of gear, the engine will stumble real quick then go away.<P>This problem started very suddenly. I was turning left at a fairly high rate of speed(stop-light was changing and I wanted through), after I completed the turn, it was jerking and bucking as I tried to go up the hill on the other side of the intersection. <P>I will go check my MAP sensor right now.<BR>Thanks for the input. Keep it coming.||||reg||1
Z||000004||2birds||06-20-2000||12:50 AM||||I'd vote for fuel delivery.<BR>How about crap in the fuel lines?  When was the fuel filter changed last?  Most fuel injected cars seem to run a little rough when the fuel level is low, maybe a litttle fuel pump cavitation, and it clears under steady speed.  Fuel meter accuracy?  How about a real injector cleaning?||||reg||1
Z||000005||ROB MAC||06-20-2000||01:17 AM||||Maybe the O2 sensor is getting ready to conk on you. Thats how mine started , then the check engine light began to come on.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Linx||06-20-2000||02:57 AM||||Im with 2birds...fuel delivery.  She's gasping for fuel, dude!  Have you run any injector cleaner thru it?<P>I dont think its the MAP sensor.  If its the MAP sensor, then disconnecting it will initiate a closed loop.  The check engine light will come on and the car will run on preset numbers.  My experience has been that, if the MAP is bad and causing the engine to run poorly, disconnecting it will force the closed loop and the engine will actually run better- giving you a shot at getting it to the dealer.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by Linx (edited 06-20-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||fiero56||06-20-2000||08:09 AM||||The car is still driveable, but going up very steep hils can be quite interesting. More information here. The car seems to hesitate more when there is more of a load on the engine, meaning it will hesitate much more easily with a little throttle at low rpm, than it does at higher throttle at higher rpm. I guess I'm saying, the hesitation is worst at low rpms with even slight throttle, but once I hit 2,500+rpm, it requires MUCH more throttle to make it stumble. Fuel delivery doesn't seem to be it, since it will run beautifully at 4,000rpm under load still. <BR>I filled the gas tank yesterday to make sure, and also replaced the MAP sensor. Problem is still there.<P>William T. III has ridden in it with this problem, and might be able to describe it a little better.||||reg||1
Z||000008||fiero56||06-20-2000||09:38 AM||||I disconnected the MAP sensor and tried to drive the car. With the sensor disconnected, it bacame almost impossible to get the car moving, undriveable for sure. It still idled perfectly, but when a load was placed on the engine, it couldn't take it and would have stalled repeatedly had I not put the clutch pedal to the floor. What does this mean?||||reg||1
Z||000009||fiero56||06-20-2000||02:02 PM||||Well, I have tried: <P>MAP sensor <BR>IAC valve<BR>TPS <BR>ignition module<P>and nothing fixed it.<P>I hooked an OTC monitor up to it, the sensors it is able to monitor look good(O2, tps).<BR>Why doesn't this thing run right?||||reg||1
Z||000010||ROB MAC||06-21-2000||01:42 AM||||Maybe the sensors look good at idle but crap out under load . Try borrowing good ones from a friendly neighbourhood mechanic like I did . It saves replacing things that don't need it. All I can say is sometimes my bad stuff did'nt show up on his scanner. My O2 didn't register bad but when I replaced it the jerking stopped.||||reg||1
Z||000011||RobH||06-21-2000||08:38 AM||||I just had a similar problem on my 85 4cyl 5spd.  Anytime I "gave it the gas" she would start to sputter and jerk, especially going up hill.<P>Changed the plug wires, and that fixed the problem.  Make sure you put dielectric grease in the boots...I forgot to do that on the wires I replaced and they got stuck on the plugs.  This may have lead to the degredation of the wires...not sure though.<P>HTH,<BR>Rob||||reg||1
Z||000012||StuGood||06-21-2000||11:22 AM||||Hmmm... What would Stu do -?  "Back to the basics" approach often works for me.  I don't think you ever said you tried changing the plug wires and plugs.<P>Not that it matters, but I had a j-e-r-k-y 1982 Chevy Cavalier that j-e-r-k-e-d every time you put it in gear and tried to take off.  When you loaded it, motor would flex in its mounts and moved enough to short out the coil 2nd-ary wire (which had a big gash in the insulation).  Shorting the coil cut torque from engine, mounts relaxed, coil wire moved back, engine got spark again, and the cycle repeated itself several times a second:<BR>J-e-r-k !  <P>Please don't be insulted - I know yours isn't a Cavalier!      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Also - for what it's worth, my 1986 SE used to have an annoying shake at highway speed.  Turned out the #4 plug wire was loose, and wouldn't snap on tightly. Changed plug wires, and problem went away.  <P>Plug wires are pretty easy to change.  And if you change the plugs, you might get a few clues by examining the condition of the old plugs.  Or, if your luck works like mine, while you're changing plugs, you'll find a shorted wire or disconnected hose, etc.      <P>Good luck!      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">   <P><p>[This message has been edited by StuGood (edited 06-21-2000).]||||reg||2
Z||000013||fiero56||06-21-2000||11:37 AM||||I have pulled each plug wire and did a visual run down on them. They all look ok. I will stick a meter on them today to make sure. They aren't even a year old, and for what payed for them($65!!!), they better still be good. I pulled the three easiest plugs, and they all look fine. WTIII brought his '85GT over, and we swapped sensors back and forth and nothing fixed it. I even bought a new ignition coil this morning(after the old one tested good), no change. I will test all the plug wires and make sure. I don't have any other ideas.||||reg||1
Z||000014||StuGood||06-21-2000||02:35 PM||||How 'bout the tach filter (my favorite subject of late!)?  Costs nothing to unplug it and try.<P>Then there's the pickup coil...<P>The way you described it happened all of a sudden one day, and the way it misses several-times-a-second, makes me think it's electrical.  Of course, my mind immediately jumps back to my previous jerky Cavalier experience...<P>Don't suppose the injector wiring could have an intermittent connection (If there was a short, I think it'd just blow the fuse)?<P>Since it doesn't cost anything, see if the injector fuses are tight in the fusebox.       <P>Now I'm really groping, but remember, you asked for all this free advice  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">! ||||reg||5
Z||000015||fiero56||06-21-2000||08:38 PM||||Free advice is the best advice. I just hate paying people to tell me: "yap, she shore ain't running right."<P>I ended up pulling each plug wire out and tested them with my multimeter(Ohms). One wire would intermitantly change readings as I twisted it. Turns out it was broken inside the housing. I can't believe it just went bad like that. No warning, no burns, cracks, or anything visuall(I pulled each one earlier, and they LOOKED good). Well, I went out, bought a new set of wires(a nice pretty red color), and the hesitation is gone! I kept dismissing the plug wires, because they were fairly new, and I am always carefull about not tugging on them. <P>You guys(girls too) are the best! I can't believe all the quesitons that have been answered here. This forum is always saving me money. Applause for everyone who helped me on this. Thank you.||||reg||7
Z||000016||StuGood||06-22-2000||08:29 AM||||"Yap - she shore runs good now."  We'll be sending you our bill  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">.||||reg||14
